Falsifies `jh7110_entropy::verification::take_serves_the_bytes_in_order_and_zeroes_them`.

The defect: `Pool::take_into`, which `Pool::take` calls, copies each byte out and leaves it in the
buffer. Every byte is still served once, because the cursor moves past it, but a served byte stays
in memory until the next refill or the drop, which is what zeroing behind the cursor exists to
prevent.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!(pool.buffer[first + j], 0, "a served byte is still in the buffer")`,
for any request of at least one byte whose served bytes were not already zero.

diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -701,5 +701,4 @@ impl Pool {
                 let at = self.cursor + i;
                 out[served + i] = self.buffer[at];
-                self.buffer[at] = 0;
             }
             self.cursor += run;
